TITLE:: PltPhase
summary:: Phase portrait of a two dimensional map
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltScatter, Classes/PltBifurcation

DESCRIPTION::
x against y, so an attractor shows as a shape rather than as a time series.

The orbit is followed for code::keep:: iterations after a code::transient::, and
stops early if it escapes, rather than plotting nonsense.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: mapFunc
Takes x and y, returns the next pair:
code::{ |x, y| [1 - (1.4 * x * x) + y, 0.3 * x] }::
argument:: x0
argument:: y0
Seeds matter: a seed off the chaotic orbit can give one point or an escape.
argument:: title
argument:: transient
argument:: keep
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: points
The computation on its own, with no window.
argument:: mapFunc
argument:: x0
argument:: y0
argument:: transient
argument:: keep
argument:: limit
Absolute value past which the orbit counts as escaped.
returns:: code::[xs, ys]::, two FloatArrays, shorter than code::keep:: if the orbit
escaped.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: seed
Rerun from a different seed, keeping the same map.

METHOD:: mapFunc
METHOD:: x0
METHOD:: y0

EXAMPLES::
code::
// Henon
PltPhase({ |x, y| [1 - (1.4 * x * x) + y, 0.3 * x] }, 0.1, 0.1, "Henon").front;

// gingerbreadman, from a seed that is on the chaotic orbit
PltPhase({ |x, y| [1 - y + x.abs, x] }, -0.1, 0, "gingerbreadman").front;

// Ikeda
(
PltPhase({ |x, y|
	var t = 0.4 - (6 / (1 + (x * x) + (y * y)));
	[1 + (0.9 * ((x * cos(t)) - (y * sin(t)))), 0.9 * ((x * sin(t)) + (y * cos(t)))]
}, 0.1, 0.1, "Ikeda").front;
)

// standard map, which is closer to isotropic, so equal aspect is worth having
(
PltPhase({ |x, y|
	var ny = y + (0.971635 * sin(x));
	[(x + ny) % 2pi, ny]
}, 0.1, 1.0, "standard map").equalAspect_(true).front;
)
::
